WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.463
The authorizing official doesn't
read the evidence directly.

2
00:00:03.463 --> 00:00:05.674
They read a package that summarizes it.

3
00:00:05.674 --> 00:00:10.618
NIST defines the package by its contents:
the security and privacy plans, the

4
00:00:10.618 --> 00:00:15.875
assessment reports, the plans of action
and milestones, and an executive summary.

5
00:00:15.875 --> 00:00:19.687
Each comes from a Risk Management
Framework task with a named owner.

6
00:00:19.687 --> 00:00:23.341
NIST also says the package
may be generated from a tool,

7
00:00:23.341 --> 00:00:28.211
and FedRAMP's 2026 agency rules
require governance and inventory tools

8
00:00:28.211 --> 00:00:33.325
that produce and ingest machine-readable
artifacts, at least OSCAL and JSON.

9
00:00:33.325 --> 00:00:36.569
OSCAL gives each artifact a
model, and the models link:

10
00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:40.846
the assessment plan imports the
security plan, the results import the

11
00:00:40.846 --> 00:00:44.975
assessment plan, and the plan of
action hangs from the security plan.

12
00:00:44.975 --> 00:00:47.513
Every failing item gets a disposition.

13
00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:51.613
Actionable drift is an open item
with an owner and a deadline.

14
00:00:51.613 --> 00:00:55.662
A governed exception carries an
in-date acceptance and its expiry.

15
00:00:55.662 --> 00:00:57.875
A lapsed acceptance reverts to open.

16
00:00:57.875 --> 00:00:59.700
Nothing is always satisfied.

17
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.774
And an empty plan of action on a Moderate
system should prompt a question:

18
00:01:03.774 --> 00:01:05.740
what didn't the assessment look at?
